## Coding Standards

<% if (language === 'typescript') { %>### TypeScript

- Strict mode always on (`"strict": true` in tsconfig)
- No `any` — use `unknown` and narrow, or create proper types
- Prefer `const` over `let`, never `var`
- Async/await over callbacks or raw Promises
- Named exports preferred over default exports
- File naming: `kebab-case.ts`<% } %><% if (language === 'rust') { %>### Rust

- All public items must have documentation comments
- Error handling: use `?` operator and typed `Result<T, E>`
- No `.unwrap()` or `.expect()` in library code; test code may use `.expect("...")`
- Use `clippy::pedantic` lint level
- Prefer `&str` over `String` in function parameters<% } %><% if (language === 'java') { %>### Java

- Hexagonal architecture: domain → no framework imports
- Use constructor injection exclusively (no field injection)
- Records for immutable data transfer objects
- No nullable fields without explicit `@Nullable` annotation
- Method length ≤ 65 LOC, cyclomatic complexity ≤ 15<% } %>

